# Plan: feed polish, cadence, and deduplication

## Modules and file organization

- **#69 UI layout:** keep the change in `frontend/src/styles.css`, alongside the
  existing `.feed-row` and `.row-actions` rules. Preserve the five-column order;
  give Actions an explicit no-wrap minimum width and let the title column absorb
  remaining width. Keep the existing narrow-screen column hiding behavior.
- **#70 worker configuration:** change the default only in
  `crates/shared/src/config.rs` (`WorkerConfig::from_env`), retaining
  `SCHEDULER_CADENCE_SECONDS` parsing as the override. Keep operator-facing
  defaults synchronized in `README.md` and `.env.example`.
- **#71 identity and persistence:** add a small, testable policy-identity helper
  in worker ingestion/change-detection code. It will prefer `(source_id,
  source_external_id)` and use normalized `(title, agency, publication_date)`
  only when a stable source identity is unavailable. Collapse a normalized batch
  before database writes, then resolve a matching persisted entry before adding
  a version. Update `crates/backend/src/feed.rs` to select one latest row per
  policy identity as a defensive read-side guard.

## Dependencies

1. #69 and #70 are independent, small changes.
2. #71 relies on the existing normalized records, `source_external_id` unique
   constraint, version persistence, and recency-first feed query. It should be
   implemented after reviewing those paths, but does not depend on #69 or #70.

## Key design decisions

- Keep deduplication deterministic: retain the first record for an identity in
  document order and log/count collapsed duplicates rather than emitting extra
  versions.
- Do not collapse genuine content revisions: after identity resolution, retain
  the existing canonical-content hash comparison so a changed policy creates a
  new version on its single entry.
- Use parameterized SQL and null-safe publication-date comparison for fallback
  matching. The feed guard should order by latest observation before choosing
  its representative, preserving the established recency-first behavior.
- Do not add broad cross-source identity guesses beyond the documented fallback;
  stable source-provided identifiers remain authoritative.
